\section{A Note on Naming Conventions}

The library follows a consistent naming scheme for feature tags. Each tag is a single CamelCase noun phrase in the \texttt{d\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}l} namespace: \texttt{P\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}p\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}l\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}e}, \texttt{O\allowbreak{}p\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}o\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}s}, \texttt{P\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}M\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}c\allowbreak{}h}, \texttt{A\allowbreak{}S\allowbreak{}T}, \texttt{R\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}w\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}e}, \texttt{E\allowbreak{}x\allowbreak{}p\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}T\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}m\allowbreak{}p\allowbreak{}l\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}s}, \texttt{C\allowbreak{}u\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}o\allowbreak{}m\allowbreak{}L\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}l\allowbreak{}s}, \texttt{M\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}m\allowbreak{}o\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}z\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}o\allowbreak{}n}, \texttt{L\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}z\allowbreak{}y\allowbreak{}F\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}u\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}e}, \texttt{M\allowbreak{}o\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}d\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}c}, \texttt{R\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}u\allowbreak{}l\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}F\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}u\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}e}, \texttt{C\allowbreak{}o\allowbreak{}m\allowbreak{}b\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}o\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}P\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}r}, \texttt{T\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}k\allowbreak{}P\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}p\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}l\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}e}. The trailing \texttt{F\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}u\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}e} suffix is used selectively, typically when the bare noun would collide with a more prominent type (for example, \texttt{L\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}z\allowbreak{}y\allowbreak{}F\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}u\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}e} avoids confusion with the \texttt{L\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}z\allowbreak{}y\allowbreak{}<\allowbreak{}T\allowbreak{}>} template of Chapter 19).

The mixin's injected members follow their own conventions. Static factories are verbs (\texttt{w\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}p}, \texttt{m\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}k\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}\_\allowbreak{}\allowbreak{}p\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}d}, \texttt{m\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}k\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}\_\allowbreak{}\allowbreak{}l\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}f}, \texttt{m\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}k\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}\_\allowbreak{}\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}o\allowbreak{}d\allowbreak{}e}, \texttt{p\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}\_\allowbreak{}\allowbreak{}l\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}l}). Instance methods that mutate derived state are also verbs (\texttt{c\allowbreak{}l\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}\_\allowbreak{}\allowbreak{}c\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}c\allowbreak{}h\allowbreak{}e}, \texttt{r\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}w\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}e}). Operators are \texttt{f\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}d} functions, never members, so that they participate in argument-dependent lookup without bloating the derived class's member set.

Users adding their own feature tags are encouraged to follow the same conventions. The two-level \texttt{T\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}g} /\allowbreak{} \texttt{M\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}x\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}<\allowbreak{}D\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}v\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}d\allowbreak{}>} shape is mandatory; the naming of injected members is up to the author, but consistency with the library's verbs-and-factories style makes user features read as if they were part of the toolkit.

\section{Combining Markers and Method-Injecting Features}

A realistic DSL frequently mixes markers with method-injecting features. A parser-DSL, for instance, might list \texttt{P\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}p\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}l\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}e} (for \texttt{w\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}p}), \texttt{A\allowbreak{}S\allowbreak{}T} (for \texttt{m\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}k\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}\_\allowbreak{}\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}o\allowbreak{}d\allowbreak{}e}), and \texttt{C\allowbreak{}o\allowbreak{}m\allowbreak{}b\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}o\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}P\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}r} (as a marker advertising that it consumes parser combinators), all at once:

\begingroup
\newminted[MDCode]{cpp}{}
\begin{MDCode}
struct ParserDSL
  : dsl::DSL<ParserDSL,
             dsl::Pipeline,
             dsl::AST,
             dsl::CombinatorParser>
{};
\end{MDCode}
\endgroup

The resulting class has \texttt{w\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}p}, \texttt{m\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}k\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}\_\allowbreak{}\allowbreak{}l\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}f}, and \texttt{m\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}k\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}\_\allowbreak{}\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}o\allowbreak{}d\allowbreak{}e} as callable members, and also satisfies \texttt{H\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}F\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}u\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}<\allowbreak{}P\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}D\allowbreak{}S\allowbreak{}L\allowbreak{},\allowbreak{}\ \allowbreak{}d\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}l\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}C\allowbreak{}o\allowbreak{}m\allowbreak{}b\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}o\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}P\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}>}. Generic parser infrastructure can therefore gate itself on the marker while ignoring the injected factories, and generic tree-building infrastructure can do the reverse.

This separation of concerns is the practical payoff of the three-category taxonomy. Markers carry intent without carrying code; method-injecting features carry code without prescribing intent; operator-injecting features carry syntax for a specific subdomain. A DSL composes the three freely.

\section{Reviewing the Conditional Contract Once More}

Because the conditional-member contract is central to two of the most useful features, it is worth restating the rules precisely.

For \texttt{R\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}w\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}w\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}e} to exist on a DSL, the derived class must define a static member named \texttt{r\allowbreak{}u\allowbreak{}l\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}s}. The member's type is expected to be a \texttt{R\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}w\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}S\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}t} (Chapter 14), but the constraint checks only the name. The body of \texttt{r\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}w\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}e} calls \texttt{D\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}v\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}d\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}u\allowbreak{}l\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}.\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}p\allowbreak{}p\allowbreak{}l\allowbreak{}y\allowbreak{}(\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{})}, so the member must in practice support that call.

For \texttt{C\allowbreak{}u\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}o\allowbreak{}m\allowbreak{}L\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}l\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}p\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}\_\allowbreak{}\allowbreak{}l\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}l} to exist on a DSL, the derived class must define a static member named \texttt{l\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}l\allowbreak{}s}. The member's type is expected to be a \texttt{L\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}l\allowbreak{}S\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}t} (Chapter 17), and the body of \texttt{p\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}\_\allowbreak{}\allowbreak{}l\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}l} calls \texttt{D\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}v\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}d\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}l\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}l\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}.\allowbreak{}p\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}(\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}p\allowbreak{}u\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{})}.

In both cases, omitting the static member does not produce a compile error at the point where the DSL is defined. The error, if any, arises only when the gated method is called or when the corresponding concept (\texttt{R\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}w\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}b\allowbreak{}l\allowbreak{}e}, \texttt{H\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}L\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}l\allowbreak{}s}) is evaluated. This deferred diagnostic is a deliberate consequence of the \texttt{r\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}q\allowbreak{}u\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}\ \allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}q\allowbreak{}u\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}s} idiom: the method is silently removed from the overload set when its precondition is unmet, rather than triggering a hard error in the mixin body.

The utility concepts exist partly to make that silent removal audible. A user who writes a generic function constrained with \texttt{R\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}w\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}b\allowbreak{}l\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}<\allowbreak{}D\allowbreak{}>} gets a clean diagnostic at the call site if \texttt{D} lacks \texttt{r\allowbreak{}u\allowbreak{}l\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}s}, instead of a deep template error inside \texttt{r\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}w\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}e}. Using the concepts is therefore the recommended way to interface with the conditional features from generic code.

\section{Putting It Together}

A feature tag is, in summary, a small struct with a nested \texttt{M\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}x\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}n} template. The tag is passed to \texttt{d\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}l\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}D\allowbreak{}S\allowbreak{}L}; the mixin is inherited. Pure markers contribute no members but enable concept-based introspection. Method-injecting features contribute static or instance member functions that typically forward to free functions in the \texttt{d\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}l} namespace. Operator-injecting features contribute \texttt{f\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}d} operators found by argument-dependent lookup. Conditional members gated on \texttt{r\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}q\allowbreak{}u\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}\ \allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}q\allowbreak{}u\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}\ \allowbreak{}\{\allowbreak{}\ \allowbreak{}D\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}v\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}d\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}m\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}m\allowbreak{}b\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{};\allowbreak{}\ \allowbreak{}\}} let a feature demand a static member from the derived class without forcing a hard error at definition time.

The utility concepts — \texttt{H\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}F\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}u\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}e}, \texttt{P\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}p\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}b\allowbreak{}l\allowbreak{}e}, \texttt{E\allowbreak{}x\allowbreak{}p\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}L\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}k\allowbreak{}e}, \texttt{H\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}L\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}l\allowbreak{}s}, \texttt{R\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}w\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}b\allowbreak{}l\allowbreak{}e} — sit on top of this machinery and let user-written templates query which features a DSL has mixed in. They are the primary interface between the feature-tag vocabulary and generic user code, and they are the reason the marker features exist at all.

The remainder of this manual examines each feature tag in detail, in the order suggested by the cross-references above. Readers who wish to write their own feature tags will find the idiom fully demystified by the illustrative \texttt{G\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}r} and \texttt{V\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}b\allowbreak{}o\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}G\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}r} examples; everything else is variation on the same two-level shape.

\section{Summary}

This chapter has catalogued the feature-tag idiom that underlies all of MuDSL's higher-level machinery. A feature tag is an empty or near-empty struct exposing a nested \texttt{t\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}m\allowbreak{}p\allowbreak{}l\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}\ \allowbreak{}<\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}y\allowbreak{}p\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}m\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}\ \allowbreak{}D\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}v\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}d\allowbreak{}>\allowbreak{}\ \allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}u\allowbreak{}c\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}\ \allowbreak{}M\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}x\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}n}, and \texttt{d\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}l\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}D\allowbreak{}S\allowbreak{}L\allowbreak{}<\allowbreak{}D\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}v\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}d\allowbreak{},\allowbreak{}\ \allowbreak{}F\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}u\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}.\allowbreak{}.\allowbreak{}.\allowbreak{}>} inherits each listed feature's \texttt{M\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}x\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}<\allowbreak{}D\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}v\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}d\allowbreak{}>} through variadic multiple inheritance.

Three categories of feature were identified. Pure markers (\texttt{P\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}M\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}c\allowbreak{}h}, \texttt{L\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}z\allowbreak{}y\allowbreak{}F\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}u\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}e}, \texttt{M\allowbreak{}o\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}d\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}c}, \texttt{R\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}u\allowbreak{}l\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}F\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}u\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}e}, \texttt{C\allowbreak{}o\allowbreak{}m\allowbreak{}b\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}o\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}P\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}r}, \texttt{T\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}k\allowbreak{}P\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}p\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}l\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}e}) inject nothing but enable concept-based introspection. Method-injecting features (\texttt{P\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}p\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}l\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}e}, \texttt{O\allowbreak{}p\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}o\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}s}, \texttt{A\allowbreak{}S\allowbreak{}T}, \texttt{M\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}m\allowbreak{}o\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}z\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}o\allowbreak{}n}, and the conditional \texttt{R\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}w\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}e} and \texttt{C\allowbreak{}u\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}o\allowbreak{}m\allowbreak{}L\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}l\allowbreak{}s}) contribute static or instance member functions. Operator-injecting features (\texttt{E\allowbreak{}x\allowbreak{}p\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}T\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}m\allowbreak{}p\allowbreak{}l\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}s}) contribute \texttt{f\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}d} operators found through argument-dependent lookup.

The conditional-member contract, expressed through \texttt{r\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}q\allowbreak{}u\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}\ \allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}q\allowbreak{}u\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}\ \allowbreak{}\{\allowbreak{}\ \allowbreak{}D\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}v\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}d\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}u\allowbreak{}l\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{};\allowbreak{}\ \allowbreak{}\}} and \texttt{r\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}q\allowbreak{}u\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}\ \allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}q\allowbreak{}u\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}\ \allowbreak{}\{\allowbreak{}\ \allowbreak{}D\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}v\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}d\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}l\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}l\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{};\allowbreak{}\ \allowbreak{}\}}, makes \texttt{r\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}w\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}e} and \texttt{p\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}\_\allowbreak{}\allowbreak{}l\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}l} available only when the derived class supplies the corresponding static member. The utility concepts \texttt{H\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}F\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}u\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}e}, \texttt{P\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}p\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}b\allowbreak{}l\allowbreak{}e}, \texttt{E\allowbreak{}x\allowbreak{}p\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}L\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}k\allowbreak{}e}, \texttt{H\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}L\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}l\allowbreak{}s}, and \texttt{R\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}w\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}b\allowbreak{}l\allowbreak{}e} mirror those contracts and provide the standard way to gate generic user code on the presence of a feature. With this vocabulary in hand, the remaining chapters examine each individual feature in turn.
